00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:08.170 align:start position:0%
Europe's massive problem of youth  unemployment tops the agenda this week  as ministers responsible for employment  Social Policy health and consumer  affairs meet in Luxembourg with EU  leaders set next week to focus on ways  to get young people into jobs the  ministers will discuss efforts to  implement a youth guarantee scheme seen  as a top EU employment priority on  average across the 27 EU nations almost  one in four people under 25 were out of  work in April the youth guarantee aims  to ensure that young people like them  receive a quality job offer further  education and apprenticeship or  traineeship within four months of  leaving school or becoming unemployed  youth unemployment will also be in the  spotlight as the ministers try to agree  a general approach on the European  globalisation adjustment fund the EGF so  that negotiations can begin with the  European Parliament's the fun was set up  in response to the economic crisis and  helps workers who lost their jobs due to  the recession and globalization to find  new employment and develop new skills  The ministers will debate the scope of  the fund ways to finance it categories  of people who might be eligible and  support for youth employment the  conditions of those most in need will  also be on the agenda as the council  hears a progress report on the fund for  European aid to the most deprived EU  leaders confirmed in February that 2.5  billion euros was earmarked for this  group to the 2014-2020 period debate  will focus on a mechanism for member  states to participate some countries  have reservations about whether an EU  level fund covering all 27 nations is  the best way to help and differ over the  form that the scheme should take
 

